1
00:00:00,449 --> 00:00:02,734
An AI agent calls a payment tool.

2
00:00:02,734 --> 00:00:04,833
The tool replies: ok.

3
00:00:04,833 --> 00:00:06,402
Did the money actually move?

4
00:00:06,402 --> 00:00:09,185
Not always. The same ok can come back when

5
00:00:09,185 --> 00:00:11,308
nothing was paid, and a timeout can hide a

6
00:00:11,308 --> 00:00:12,816
payment that did go through.

7
00:00:13,616 --> 00:00:15,818
We built ActionProof to measure this.

8
00:00:15,818 --> 00:00:18,979
On 440 locked tasks, an agent that trusts the

9
00:00:18,979 --> 00:00:21,950
reply falsely reports success 86 percent of the

10
00:00:21,950 --> 00:00:22,692
time.

11
00:00:23,492 --> 00:00:26,051
So here is the real project, running live:

12
00:00:26,051 --> 00:00:28,068
our Space, with our trained three billion

13
00:00:28,068 --> 00:00:30,860
parameter Cohere agent on an L4 GPU.

14
00:00:30,860 --> 00:00:33,855
The task: order seventeen safety gloves, prepay

15
00:00:33,855 --> 00:00:36,308
the supplier, and post the journal entry.

16
00:00:36,308 --> 00:00:38,336
The reality, hidden from the agent:

17
00:00:38,336 --> 00:00:40,117
the payment will silently fail.

18
00:00:40,117 --> 00:00:40,871
Run.

19
00:00:41,671 --> 00:00:44,619
On the left, a script that trusts every reply:

20
00:00:44,619 --> 00:00:46,904
three oks, and it reports done.

21
00:00:46,904 --> 00:00:49,180
On the right, our model, live.

22
00:00:49,180 --> 00:00:51,338
It places the order, and checks it.

23
00:00:51,338 --> 00:00:52,765
It pays the supplier.

24
00:00:52,765 --> 00:00:55,217
The tool says ok. Then it reads the payment

25
00:00:55,217 --> 00:00:58,142
ledger. Empty. The ok was a lie.

26
00:00:58,142 --> 00:01:00,642
So it pays again, with the same idempotency

27
00:01:00,642 --> 00:01:03,012
key, so a real first payment could never be

28
00:01:03,012 --> 00:01:05,312
charged twice. It reads again:

29
00:01:05,312 --> 00:01:06,762
exactly one payment.

30
00:01:06,762 --> 00:01:09,475
It posts the journal entry, checks that too,

31
00:01:09,475 --> 00:01:11,337
and only then reports success.

32
00:01:12,137 --> 00:01:14,189
Only after the run does the Space reveal the

33
00:01:14,189 --> 00:01:16,627
hidden databases. The script:

34
00:01:16,627 --> 00:01:19,056
false success, and no payment at all.

35
00:01:19,056 --> 00:01:22,393
Our agent: goal met, exactly one payment, and

36
00:01:22,393 --> 00:01:24,268
every step verified against the system of

37
00:01:24,268 --> 00:01:25,034
record.

38
00:01:25,834 --> 00:01:27,638
But training that habit exposed something

39
00:01:27,638 --> 00:01:29,640
deeper about how we train agents.

40
00:01:29,640 --> 00:01:31,916
With standard GRPO, the agent should learn to

41
00:01:31,916 --> 00:01:34,074
check whenever checking is worth its cost.

42
00:01:34,074 --> 00:01:35,701
Often, it does not.

43
00:01:35,701 --> 00:01:37,470
And the cause is not the reward.

44
00:01:37,470 --> 00:01:40,182
It is how rewards are scaled inside the group.

45
00:01:40,982 --> 00:01:43,600
In GRPO, all the rollouts of one task share the

46
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:46,371
same hidden world: the same database, the same

47
00:01:46,371 --> 00:01:48,939
silent fault. Rewards are then standardised

48
00:01:48,939 --> 00:01:51,297
inside that group. That division cancels each

49
00:01:51,297 --> 00:01:53,432
hidden state's stakes, how much a missed fault

50
00:01:53,432 --> 00:01:56,250
actually costs. What survives is a vote:

51
00:01:56,250 --> 00:01:58,986
which states prefer checking, not by how much.

52
00:01:58,986 --> 00:02:01,686
So a rare, expensive fault can be outvoted by a

53
00:02:01,686 --> 00:02:03,749
cheap, common clean case.

54
00:02:04,549 --> 00:02:06,554
We registered the predictions before testing

55
00:02:06,554 --> 00:02:09,499
them, on 731 held-out cells.

56
00:02:09,499 --> 00:02:13,001
The theory predicts what GRPO learns in all 721

57
00:02:13,001 --> 00:02:16,268
decided cells, including all 77 where the vote

58
00:02:16,268 --> 00:02:17,695
and the value disagree.

59
00:02:17,695 --> 00:02:20,230
One registered hypothesis failed, and one could

60
00:02:20,230 --> 00:02:22,741
not be tested. We report both.

61
00:02:23,541 --> 00:02:25,239
Then we tested it on the agent you just

62
00:02:25,239 --> 00:02:27,253
watched, five seeds per arm.

63
00:02:27,253 --> 00:02:30,071
Every GRPO seed ends exactly where it started:

64
00:02:30,071 --> 00:02:32,265
it never reads. Remove the group standard

65
00:02:32,265 --> 00:02:35,095
deviation, and the same model learns to check:

66
00:02:35,095 --> 00:02:40,519
verified completion rises from 0.687 to 0.807.

67
00:02:40,519 --> 00:02:41,923
Plus twelve points.

68
00:02:41,923 --> 00:02:43,798
And we say what this does not show:

69
00:02:43,798 --> 00:02:46,604
the GRPO arm is degenerate, so the p value is

70
00:02:46,604 --> 00:02:47,771
the test's floor.

71
00:02:48,571 --> 00:02:50,729
The proofs, the registrations and the limits

72
00:02:50,729 --> 00:02:52,131
are all in the paper.

73
00:02:52,931 --> 00:02:54,901
And every number you have seen resolves to one

74
00:02:54,901 --> 00:02:56,454
file and one command.

75
00:02:56,454 --> 00:03:00,122
A fresh public clone re-derived all 899 claims,

76
00:03:00,122 --> 00:03:02,821
with zero failures, and no GPU.

77
00:03:03,621 --> 00:03:06,354
ActionProof. When you train agents to check,

78
00:03:06,354 --> 00:03:07,780
keep the stakes in the group.
